{"vid":"NtNkugcdDYM","source":"NtNkugcdDYM.en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":0.7,"txt":"(lively music) (screen whooshing)"},{"t":4.9,"txt":"- [Narrator] In shaft alignment,"},{"t":6.3,"txt":"the shaft of the driven machine"},{"t":8.1,"txt":"is typically considered stationary"},{"t":10.2,"txt":"while the other, the shaft of the driver, is movable."},{"t":14.2,"txt":"The movable shaft is adjusted in relation"},{"t":16.6,"txt":"to the stationary shaft to bring both shafts into alignment"},{"t":20.5,"txt":"and to satisfy machinery alignment tolerances."},{"t":24.7,"txt":"In this video, we'll be alternating between 3D view"},{"t":28.1,"txt":"and graphical view."},{"t":31,"txt":"In the graphical view, the horizontal length"},{"t":33.2,"txt":"of each square represents one inch,"},{"t":35.5,"txt":"while the vertical length of each square"},{"t":37.2,"txt":"represents five one thousandths of an inch."},{"t":41.3,"txt":"While we'll only be working in the vertical plane,"},{"t":44.3,"txt":"the same concepts also apply to the horizontal plane."},{"t":49.1,"txt":"Before we start, let's measure"},{"t":51.3,"txt":"and indicate in our graph the positions of the front"},{"t":54.2,"txt":"and back feet of the stationary machine,"},{"t":56.8,"txt":"(pen scratching)"},{"t":57.7,"txt":"the center of the coupling, and the front"},{"t":59.9,"txt":"and back feet of the movable machine."},{"t":63.5,"txt":"Beginning with our shafts in perfect alignment,"},{"t":66.5,"txt":"let's introduce some offset misalignment"},{"t":69.2,"txt":"by raising the front and back feet"},{"t":71.2,"txt":"of the movable machine equally by 20 thou."},{"t":75.2,"txt":"The movable shaft center line is now offset"},{"t":77.9,"txt":"from the stationary shaft center line"},{"t":80.3,"txt":"by 20 thou at every point along its length."},{"t":84,"txt":"Because we've added the same amount to both front"},{"t":86.3,"txt":"and back feet, the shafts are parallel."},{"t":89.8,"txt":"There is no angular misalignment."},{"t":92.7,"txt":"Our offset misalignment is 20 and our angularity is zero."},{"t":99,"txt":"(screen whooshing)"},{"t":100.3,"txt":"In our next example, we'll induce both offset"},{"t":103.3,"txt":"and angular misalignment by raising the back feet by 20 thou"},{"t":108.2,"txt":"while leaving the front feet where they are."},{"t":111.2,"txt":"Now, the movable shaft center line is sitting 20 thou high"},{"t":115.2,"txt":"at the back feet, while still sitting at zero thou"},{"t":118.4,"txt":"of offset at the front feet."},{"t":121.4,"txt":"So what is our angularity?"},{"t":124.6,"txt":"In geometry, this concept is called slope or rise over run."},{"t":130.4,"txt":"Angularity or slope expresses the amount of rise"},{"t":133.8,"txt":"or fall of the movable shaft over each unit"},{"t":136.5,"txt":"of measurement along the shaft's length."},{"t":139.5,"txt":"Angularity is expressed as thousandths per inch."},{"t":143.9,"txt":"From the graph, we can see that the shaft rises 20 thou"},{"t":147.4,"txt":"between the front and back feet of the motor."},{"t":150.3,"txt":"The distance between the front"},{"t":151.8,"txt":"and back feet of the motor is 10 blocks across or 10 inches."},{"t":157.4,"txt":"Divide the rise of 20 thou by the run of 10 inches"},{"t":161.6,"txt":"to get the angularity, which is two thou per inch."},{"t":168.4,"txt":"Now what about the offset?"},{"t":170.9,"txt":"As you can see from the graph,"},{"t":172.7,"txt":"the offset is now a different amount at every point"},{"t":175.8,"txt":"along the shaft's center lines."},{"t":178.1,"txt":"You might ask why the offset"},{"t":179.7,"txt":"is expressed as a single number?"},{"t":182.8,"txt":"It is because there is a standard reference point"},{"t":185.3,"txt":"for offset misalignment at the plane of power transmission,"},{"t":189.6,"txt":"typically, at the center of the coupling."},{"t":193.7,"txt":"The graph shows that the offset is 20 thou at the back feet"},{"t":197.4,"txt":"and zero thou at the front feet."},{"t":200.5,"txt":"We know that the offset changes by two thou"},{"t":203.2,"txt":"for every inch we move along the shaft."},{"t":205.8,"txt":"The coupling center is four inches from the front feet."},{"t":209.3,"txt":"Over those four inches,"},{"t":210.9,"txt":"the movable shaft will drop an additional eight thou,"},{"t":214.4,"txt":"four inches times two thou."},{"t":217.2,"txt":"Therefore, the offset at the coupling center"},{"t":219.8,"txt":"is negative eight thou."},{"t":222.8,"txt":"In this example, the movable shaft is sitting low"},{"t":225.8,"txt":"at the coupling, but high at the rear feet."},{"t":228.7,"txt":"At this point, we might hear an aligner ask,"},{"t":231.3,"txt":"\"I can see that the shaft is low at the coupling."},{"t":234.2,"txt":"Why is the laser telling me to lower the feet"},{"t":236.8,"txt":"if the shaft is already low at the coupling?\""},{"t":240.5,"txt":"Careful examination of this graph provides the answer."},{"t":244.6,"txt":"Since we induce the angular misalignment by adding 20 thou"},{"t":247.7,"txt":"to the back feet, the only way"},{"t":249.7,"txt":"to correct the misalignment is by removing 20 thou"},{"t":253,"txt":"to lower the rear feet."},{"t":254.6,"txt":"(lively music) (screen whooshing)"},{"t":257.6,"txt":"Subscribe to the Acoem USA YouTube channel today"},{"t":260.6,"txt":"to receive notice of new videos."},{"t":262.7,"txt":"(lively music ending)"}],"words":[{"t":0.673,"w":"(lively"},{"t":0.673,"w":"music)"},{"t":0.673,"w":"(screen"},{"t":0.673,"w":"whooshing)"},{"t":4.89,"w":"-"},{"t":4.89,"w":"[Narrator]"},{"t":4.89,"w":"In"},{"t":4.89,"w":"shaft"},{"t":4.89,"w":"alignment,"},{"t":6.27,"w":"the"},{"t":6.27,"w":"shaft"},{"t":6.27,"w":"of"},{"t":6.27,"w":"the"},{"t":6.27,"w":"driven"},{"t":6.27,"w":"machine"},{"t":8.13,"w":"is"},{"t":8.13,"w":"typically"},{"t":8.13,"w":"considered"},{"t":8.13,"w":"stationary"},{"t":10.17,"w":"while"},{"t":10.17,"w":"the"},{"t":10.17,"w":"other,"},{"t":10.17,"w":"the"},{"t":10.17,"w":"shaft"},{"t":10